<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lech Wałęsa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">b. <a href="/years/1943">1943</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Lech Walesa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lech Wałęsa is a Polish trade unionist and statesman who led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solidarity-polish-trade-union">Solidarity</a> movement and was president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> from 1990 to 1995. An electrician at the Lenin Shipyard in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gdansk">Gdańsk</a>, he was dismissed in 1976 for his union activity. In August 1980 he led the strike committee that negotiated the Gdańsk Agreement, which permitted an independent trade union, and he became chairman of Solidarity. Imprisoned under martial law in 1981–1982, he received the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-peace-prize">Nobel Peace Prize</a> in 1983 and in 1989 informally led the opposition side in the Round Table talks.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lech-walesa.jpg" alt="Lech Wałęsa" width="320" height="422" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: MEDEF · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lech_Walesa_-_2009.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In Poland in 1980 the crane operator Anna Walentynowicz, whose dismissal set off the Gdańsk strike, and the electrician <b>Lech Wałęsa</b> stood at the head of the movement that became Solidarity.</blockquote><a href="/movement/labour-movement">Labour movement · People</a></li><li><blockquote>That same year, 1979, Pope John Paul II, a native of Poland, visited the country; a figure Poles could identify with, beyond the regime’s reach, he was later named by <b>Lech Wałęsa</b> as a significant factor in Solidarity’s creation.</blockquote><a href="/movement/solidarity">Solidarity · Background</a></li></ul></section>
